<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rudolph of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/936">936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Raoul of France, Rudolph of West Francia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rudolph was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 921 and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> from 923 until his death in 936, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-the-justiciar">Richard the Justiciar</a>. Elected by the nobles in place of the deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a>, he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> and struggled to win recognition in the south. He died without children, and the Carolingian Louis IV was recalled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudolph-of-france.jpg" alt="Rudolph of France" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grandes Chroniques de France · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Rudolph_of_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
